00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:25.351
- I'm Merv.

00:08:25.351 --> 00:08:27.974
Fantasy Sports, the daily
fantasy sports market

00:08:27.974 --> 00:08:29.111
is blowing up lately.

00:08:29.451 --> 00:08:31.510
Draft Kings bought by Disney.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.110
FanDuel, partnering with NBC Sports.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:35.787
How do you think that
changes the game for us

00:08:35.787 --> 00:08:37.476
as fans and as marketers?

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:39.217
Does it shift us more
to being fans of players

00:08:39.217 --> 00:08:43.362
instead of teams, and is loyalty gone?
- Ugh, Merv.

00:08:43.362 --> 00:08:45.510
That's a good question!

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:51.772
So, if Tom Brady's leg fell
off his body today in the news,

00:08:51.772 --> 00:08:53.642
I'd be happy, right?

00:08:53.642 --> 00:08:54.669
I'm more than happy to say that.

00:08:54.669 --> 00:08:56.910
I understand how that's
disgusting as a human being,

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:57.931
but it's the truth.

00:08:57.931 --> 00:08:59.623
So I'm just telling the truth.

00:08:59.623 --> 00:09:02.230
If tomorrow Tom Brady
got traded to the Jets,

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.328
I would love him with all my heart.

00:09:04.328 --> 00:09:06.964
So you're talking to somebody
who completely and utterly

00:09:06.964 --> 00:09:11.455
100% all in, only roots for
the logo, not the player.

00:09:11.455 --> 00:09:15.333
Video games, sports memorabilia,
and now more than ever,

00:09:15.333 --> 00:09:18.050
Fantasy Sports has
clearly created a culture.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:22.266
I had a family event, unfortunately
a negative family event,

00:09:23.436 --> 00:09:25.912
Liz's 99-year-old Grandmother passed away,

00:09:25.912 --> 00:09:30.204
we had a ceremony, and Liz's
cousin was there, he's young.

00:09:30.204 --> 00:09:31.969
And he's a Seahawks fan.

00:09:31.969 --> 00:09:34.684
And we're sitting in the
living room and Sports Center

00:09:34.684 --> 00:09:36.252
comes up and Peyton Manning comes up

00:09:36.252 --> 00:09:37.274
and he's like, "Yeah, Peyton!"

00:09:37.274 --> 00:09:38.284
He's like nine or 11.

00:09:38.284 --> 00:09:40.683
"Yeah Peyton Manning, the man,
I love you Peyton Manning!"

00:09:40.683 --> 00:09:42.037
And I looked at him and said,

00:09:42.037 --> 00:09:44.251
"You're a (beep) Seahawks fan!

00:09:44.251 --> 00:09:46.131
"Like, you played each
other in the Super Bowl."

00:09:46.131 --> 00:09:47.386
He's like, "Yeah, but
I love Peyton Manning,

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:48.407
fantasy, five-touch --"

00:09:48.407 --> 00:09:50.032
I was like this sucks.

00:09:50.032 --> 00:09:52.389
I hate the culture -- like for me

00:09:52.389 --> 00:09:54.769
by the way, this whole
show for 116 episodes

00:09:54.769 --> 00:09:57.450
I say, "Don't be romantic,
don't be romantic

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:58.983
don't be romantic."

00:09:58.983 --> 00:10:00.504
And that's because I think this is

00:10:00.504 --> 00:10:01.989
like it's a very deadly sin.

00:10:01.989 --> 00:10:04.149
In sports, I am outrageously romantic.

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:05.971
It's the one place where I think

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:08.093
I'm the worst version of myself.

00:10:08.093 --> 00:10:09.942
And it is stunning for me

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:12.170
how much I hate player culture

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.522
where like you are a Giants fan,

00:10:14.522 --> 00:10:16.385
but you like a Cowboy player?

00:10:16.385 --> 00:10:17.383
It makes no sense to me.

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:20.533
I've been in environments, one
of the great things that has

00:10:20.533 --> 00:10:23.229
ever happened to me on this
issue was, I was at a sports bar

00:10:23.229 --> 00:10:25.501
before a Jets game, because I
can never be in a bar setting

00:10:25.501 --> 00:10:28.101
for a Jets game, but before a Jets game,

00:10:28.101 --> 00:10:31.217
there was Giants fan wearing
an Eli Manning Jersey

00:10:31.657 --> 00:10:34.507
and the Giants were killing the Eagles.

00:10:34.507 --> 00:10:37.583
And a random touch-down
by DeSean Jackson happens

00:10:37.583 --> 00:10:40.303
and the Giants fan goes, "Yeah!"

00:10:40.647 --> 00:10:45.059
And I looked at him and said,
"You are a piece of (beep)."

00:10:45.059 --> 00:10:46.406
I'm like how in the world did he --

00:10:46.406 --> 00:10:47.821
He's like, "DeSean's on my fantasy team."

00:10:47.821 --> 00:10:50.701
So, this was a game several years ago

00:10:50.701 --> 00:10:54.497
where the Eagles had a furious
come-back in the 4th quarter,

00:10:54.497 --> 00:10:56.598
and DeSean Jackson had
the kick-off and scored

00:10:56.598 --> 00:10:58.642
and the Eagles came back and won that game

00:10:58.642 --> 00:11:01.575
after being down by like three touchdowns.

00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:03.809
I was so happy.

00:11:04.202 --> 00:11:07.545
A happiness that I can't even explain that

00:11:07.545 --> 00:11:11.614
that guy got it stuck to him
for cheering for a fantasy

00:11:11.614 --> 00:11:13.164
thing over his favorite football team.

00:11:13.164 --> 00:11:14.383
But that's something I'm romantic about,

00:11:14.383 --> 00:11:15.498
so how do I think?

00:11:15.498 --> 00:11:17.599
I think most of the kids these days

00:11:17.599 --> 00:11:19.613
and even the grownups are getting re-wired

00:11:19.613 --> 00:11:21.197
to be more player-centric.

00:11:21.197 --> 00:11:23.620
I think that's really
dangerous for the leagues.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:24.878
You wanna talk about
something that could happen

00:11:24.878 --> 00:11:26.921
in 10 to 20 to 30 years?

00:11:26.921 --> 00:11:29.433
I truly believe before I die,

00:11:29.433 --> 00:11:33.062
the five to 15 most influential
athletes within a league

00:11:33.062 --> 00:11:36.005
will go out and start a separate
league, and pull it off.

00:11:36.005 --> 00:11:36.940
Before I die.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.334
I believe that will happen.

00:11:38.334 --> 00:11:40.123
And I think that's super interesting.

00:11:40.123 --> 00:11:42.686
Tie in question one to
question three on this episode.

00:11:42.686 --> 00:11:43.533
Because I think that they're

00:11:43.533 --> 00:11:45.843
gaining disproportionate leverage.

00:11:45.843 --> 00:11:49.590
The cost of entry, of distribution
TV rights is changing.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:52.336
And I think there will
be smart business people

00:11:52.336 --> 00:11:54.506
that will come in and pay
them to go start that league

00:11:54.506 --> 00:11:56.735
and change the dynamics.
- Sure.

00:11:56.735 --> 00:11:58.659
- I think it's a very
slippery slope for the leagues

00:11:58.659 --> 00:12:00.576
to go down this path.

00:12:00.576 --> 00:12:02.259
But they will because
the money is short-term

00:12:02.259 --> 00:12:03.770
and they'll take it.

00:12:03.987 --> 00:12:05.251
Cool man, thanks for your question.

00:12:05.251 --> 00:12:05.904
- [Group] (applause)

00:12:05.904 --> 00:12:06.612
- Hey!

00:12:06.612 --> 00:12:07.297
- [Kim] Hi Gary!
- How are ya?

00:12:07.297 --> 00:12:08.970
- Good how are you?
- Good.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.953
- Kim Francis, month two at VaynerMedia.

00:12:11.953 --> 00:12:13.971
- Yes, Merv, you're also month what, one,

00:12:13.971 --> 00:12:15.381
- [Merv] One and a half.
- two, one and a half?

00:12:15.381 --> 00:12:16.376
- Some newbies on the show!

00:12:16.376 --> 00:12:17.774
- Yes, absolutely.

00:12:17.774 --> 00:12:19.500
So one of things that I love
the most about being here

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.135
is the hustle way of life.

00:12:21.135 --> 00:12:22.113
I think it's amazing.

00:12:22.113 --> 00:12:23.180
- Okay.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:26.995
- What are your suggestions
when working with external

00:12:26.995 --> 00:12:30.077
partners, who you gotta work
with to get the job done,

00:12:30.077 --> 00:12:31.780
who don't share your hustle?

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.669
- You mean like the rest
of the entire world?

00:12:34.669 --> 00:12:36.796
- Yes, them. Exactly.

00:12:37.906 --> 00:12:41.128
- From an agency dynamic,
we're stuck, right?

00:12:41.128 --> 00:12:42.728
You wanna look good in the sandbox

00:12:42.728 --> 00:12:44.828
which for people that don't
understand, you work with

00:12:44.828 --> 00:12:47.638
a brand and you've got
four, five, six, three

00:12:47.638 --> 00:12:49.577
different agency partners
and it's important that

00:12:49.577 --> 00:12:52.871
what a brand hates that we
work with is when the agencies

00:12:52.871 --> 00:12:54.895
are playing politics
with each other because

00:12:54.895 --> 00:12:57.831
their assumption is that we as
Vayner, are trying to get the

00:12:57.831 --> 00:13:00.176
money of this person,
and that makes sense.

00:13:00.176 --> 00:13:01.209
That's a cynical point of view,

00:13:01.209 --> 00:13:02.486
that's the right point of view

00:13:02.486 --> 00:13:04.436
by the way, 90% of the time.

00:13:04.436 --> 00:13:07.340
And so, I, and you may not
fully know this but some of the

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:08.902
people that have been
here a little bit longer,

00:13:08.902 --> 00:13:11.472
I'm actually very aggressive,
like don't go down that route,

00:13:11.472 --> 00:13:14.460
we'll get ours by just
showing what we're about.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.034
But it makes it frustrating for all of you

00:13:16.034 --> 00:13:16.905
that are in the trenches

00:13:16.905 --> 00:13:19.181
because I'm probably taking,
I'm probably pushing it

00:13:19.181 --> 00:13:21.630
even too far of like
"be nice to everybody",

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:22.872
even though they suck.

00:13:22.872 --> 00:13:24.272
Or you think they suck.

00:13:24.972 --> 00:13:26.314
- I don't think they suck, I just said

00:13:26.314 --> 00:13:27.771
they don't hustle.
- I get it, I think everyone sucks.

00:13:27.771 --> 00:13:31.082
And so, I think that

00:13:32.742 --> 00:13:34.469
it's a balancing act.

00:13:34.469 --> 00:13:36.071
It's a really fine, tight rope.

00:13:36.071 --> 00:13:39.679
I think letting your work speak for itself

00:13:39.679 --> 00:13:40.993
is a very smart strategy.

00:13:40.993 --> 00:13:43.246
I think the truth is undefeated.

00:13:43.246 --> 00:13:46.148
Meaning, I do think eventually
the truth bubbles up right?

00:13:46.148 --> 00:13:49.568
Now, you may have a
flawed judge of the truth.

00:13:49.568 --> 00:13:52.493
Like there's a human
being that runs that brand

00:13:52.493 --> 00:13:54.356
that may not see it, maybe tricked,

00:13:54.356 --> 00:13:56.934
may have too much romance
for the prior world.

00:13:56.934 --> 00:13:59.287
Or logos over team players.

00:13:59.287 --> 00:14:03.501
And so, I think you have to
assess who the judge and jury is

00:14:04.153 --> 00:14:06.671
number one because that's
just the real game.

00:14:07.031 --> 00:14:09.487
I think you need to build
a relationship, for real,

00:14:09.487 --> 00:14:12.291
outside of what's happening
in the room with those people.

00:14:12.291 --> 00:14:14.611
Because let's not forget they're
just humans that work for

00:14:14.611 --> 00:14:17.029
a company, and I have
found that a lot of times

00:14:17.029 --> 00:14:18.923
they realize they stink too.

00:14:18.923 --> 00:14:19.885
Because maybe their company stinks

00:14:19.885 --> 00:14:22.428
and at least that gets you
aligned where you're not mad

00:14:22.428 --> 00:14:23.761
at the person, you're mad at a logo.

00:14:23.761 --> 00:14:26.200
Which I think often times
takes a lot of venom

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.770
out of the situation,
makes it all more palpable.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:30.433
I think what it comes down to

00:14:30.433 --> 00:14:33.135
is good old-fashioned
communication, on all levels.

00:14:33.135 --> 00:14:33.831
Your own team.

00:14:33.831 --> 00:14:35.471
As somebody who's a little more senior.

00:14:35.471 --> 00:14:37.240
One of the things that we
struggle with here at Vayner

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.039
is the youngsters don't
fully get it all the way

00:14:39.039 --> 00:14:40.281
they haven't been through it.

00:14:40.281 --> 00:14:41.685
So they're looking just at the narrow

00:14:41.685 --> 00:14:44.448
like this project isn't done
at Thursday at 4, they stink!

00:14:44.448 --> 00:14:46.480
There's a lot more going on than that.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:49.695
So I think communication
with your team, to the side,

00:14:49.695 --> 00:14:51.221
the different players, the client.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.684
Just communication,
communication, communication.

00:14:53.684 --> 00:14:55.604
- I like it, thank you!
- Cool, you got it.

00:14:55.604 --> 00:14:58.285
- [Group] (applause)

00:14:58.285 --> 00:15:00.841
- Very selfish question I would say.

00:15:00.841 --> 00:15:03.195
I'm just kidding, I'm
kidding, I'm kidding.

00:15:03.195 --> 00:15:03.963
I'm not moving DRock.

00:15:03.963 --> 00:15:04.996
- How's it going Gary?

00:15:04.996 --> 00:15:08.518
- You move mother (beep).
- [Group] (laughs)

00:15:09.088 --> 00:15:12.493
- Vayner Nation, my name's
Jody Gowen, nice to meet you.

00:15:12.493 --> 00:15:14.620
- Nice to meet you.
- I've already met you.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:17.771
Alright so, we talk a lot about
press and the pros and cons

00:15:17.771 --> 00:15:21.620
of getting press, but even
in like bad press situations

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:24.322
someone, like some troll will
always bring up the argument

00:15:24.322 --> 00:15:26.179
like, "Well, at least we're
talking about it, right?"

00:15:26.179 --> 00:15:29.015
- Right, the thought of
all press is good press?

00:15:29.015 --> 00:15:31.344
- Yeah, what's your thoughts
on that, agree or disagree?

00:15:31.344 --> 00:15:32.775
- Fundamentally disagree.

00:15:32.775 --> 00:15:36.821
Like if somebody said you're a
murderer and a child molester

00:15:36.821 --> 00:15:39.447
if you think that's good, if
you think that's good press,

00:15:39.447 --> 00:15:41.077
you're out of your (beep) mind, right?

00:15:41.077 --> 00:15:43.164
So like the thought that all press

00:15:43.164 --> 00:15:45.260
is good press is ludicrous.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.102
Everything matters contextually.

00:15:47.102 --> 00:15:50.656
It's the same reason I feel
crazy about media in our world.

00:15:50.656 --> 00:15:53.436
Everybody thinks all
impressions are equal.

00:15:53.436 --> 00:15:55.662
They're not, like the content matters.

00:15:55.662 --> 00:15:58.316
Like the way people
feel about you matters.

00:15:58.316 --> 00:16:01.834
If they think, I'm adamantly against that

00:16:01.834 --> 00:16:03.877
and I think that's, I
think the people that say

00:16:03.877 --> 00:16:08.312
all press is good press are
people that get bad press.

00:16:08.312 --> 00:16:10.977
Like, I really do.

00:16:10.977 --> 00:16:14.534
I can't wrap my head around
bad press being a good thing.

00:16:14.534 --> 00:16:15.614
Like. how?

00:16:15.614 --> 00:16:18.156
That completely does the thing
that pisses me the most off

00:16:18.156 --> 00:16:21.705
about media, it takes the
context out of the equation.

00:16:21.705 --> 00:16:23.276
Well you still reached 11 million people.

00:16:23.276 --> 00:16:24.384
Like really?

00:16:24.384 --> 00:16:26.305
Donald Trump's getting a
(beep)-load of press right now,

00:16:26.305 --> 00:16:27.932
do we think that's good?

00:16:27.932 --> 00:16:30.113
Who could possibly think that's good?

00:16:30.113 --> 00:16:32.157
That's a crazy thought.

00:16:32.157 --> 00:16:35.930
And so, I think that's a massive misnomer.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.890
And I'm very disrespectful
to anybody that believes

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:40.376
that to be true, Jody.

00:16:40.376 --> 00:16:42.301
- Alright, thank you!

00:16:42.301 --> 00:16:42.872
- You got it.

00:16:42.872 --> 00:16:46.267
- [Group] (applause)

00:16:46.888 --> 00:16:47.968
- Didn't think child molester

00:16:47.968 --> 00:16:50.269
was gonna get dropped on this episode.

00:16:50.696 --> 00:16:52.039
We have a bonus question, let's go.

00:16:52.039 --> 00:16:53.331
Why is Allison the bonus question?

00:16:53.331 --> 00:16:55.362
- [India] Because it's fun.
- OK.

00:16:55.362 --> 00:16:57.503
- Because it's my only question
I'm scheduling a one-on-one.

00:16:57.503 --> 00:16:58.913
- Got it!
- [Group] (laughs)

00:16:59.963 --> 00:17:01.243
- I'm Allison, I'm very nervous.

00:17:01.243 --> 00:17:04.372
I don't really know, I have
no idea what I'm about to ask

00:17:04.372 --> 00:17:05.207
or what's gonna come out of my mouth.

00:17:05.207 --> 00:17:08.087
- I'm excited, I love improv.

00:17:09.117 --> 00:17:11.363
- Shoot, oh my gosh.

00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:13.833
What was either your first

00:17:13.833 --> 00:17:16.258
or your most embarrassing screen name?

00:17:16.758 --> 00:17:18.595
- Oh!
- [Group] (laughs)

00:17:20.704 --> 00:17:24.711
One of my AOL chat names
way back in the day

00:17:24.711 --> 00:17:26.731
was GeeNutz with a Z.

00:17:26.731 --> 00:17:28.391
- (laughs)

00:17:28.391 --> 00:17:30.910
- G-E-E N-U-T-Z.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:32.674
- (laughs)

00:17:32.674 --> 00:17:35.844
- That one is a good one.

00:17:37.484 --> 00:17:39.253
That is your answer Allison.

00:17:39.253 --> 00:17:40.650
Not bad for an improvisation.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.368
Good job, awesome.
- Yeah it's great.

00:17:42.368 --> 00:17:44.029
Let's clap that up.
- [Group] (applause)

00:17:44.029 --> 00:17:45.928
Is that it, India, that's it?

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:49.753
Oh man, appreciate the live audience.

00:17:49.753 --> 00:17:52.922
Give a little camera action for everybody.

00:17:54.372 --> 00:17:55.289
I appreciate it guys,

00:17:55.289 --> 00:17:58.116
hope you guys all have
a good Fourth of July.

00:18:00.304 --> 00:18:04.146
GeeNutz signing off
for the Fourth of July,

00:18:04.146 --> 00:18:08.102
question of the day, how
badly do you wanna work here?

00:18:08.102 --> 00:18:11.728
You keep asking questions,
I'll keep answering them.

00:18:11.728 --> 00:18:13.952
Thanks guys.
- [Group] (applause)

